   "path": "SCENE.md",
   "role": "instructions",
   "content": "# Shader Grain Gradient\n\nA grainy multi-stop gradient shape drifts slowly under film-grain texture: an analog, premium WebGL backdrop in any palette and one of seven shapes.\n\nCategory: shaders. Length: 5 s. WebGL: yes.\n\nUse when:\n- A gradient backdrop needs analog texture: the grain reads as film or print rather than flat digital.\n- The tone is premium and editorial and the motion should stay subtle.\n- The field should be quieter: raise `softness` and `noise`.\n\nAvoid when:\n- You need crisp, clean color with no texture: use shader-mesh-gradient.\n- Foreground text is tiny: heavy grain lowers contrast, so lower `intensity` and `noise` or use a solid backdrop.\n- You want structured geometry: use shader-dot-orbit or shader-voronoi.\n- You want a finished look without choosing colors: use a preset such as shader-grain-gradient-sunrise.\n\nVariables:\n- colors (string): Colors, default \"#3a3a52, #4a4a68, #5a5a7e\"\n- color_back (color): Background, default \"#12121a\"\n- shape (enum): Shape, default \"corners\"\n- speed (number, 0-4): Speed, default 1\n- softness (number, 0-1): Softness, default 0.6\n- intensity (number, 0-1): Intensity, default 0.2\n- noise (number, 0-1): Noise, default 0.15\n- rotation (number, 0-360): Rotation, default 0\n- scale (number, 0.1-4): Scale, default 1\n- resolution (number, 0.25-1): Resolution, default 1\n\nSource: https://github.com/Remocn/remocn. Licence: MIT. The scene file keeps the upstream notice.\n"
  },
